<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li><li aria-current="page">The Mississippi Appraisal Clause, Read Line by Line</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Claim advice · 8 min read</p><h1>The Mississippi Appraisal Clause, Read Line by Line</h1><p class="lede">What the typical appraisal clause in a Mississippi property policy says, how each step works, and where its limits are.</p></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Mississippi Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-08-24">August 24, 2026</time> · 8 min read</p><h2>Where appraisal rights come from in Mississippi</h2><p>Mississippi doesn’t have a special appraisal statute that spells out how property appraisals must run. Your right to appraisal comes from the policy itself, usually a short paragraph titled “Appraisal” in the conditions section of a homeowners, wind, condo or commercial form. That means the rules for your claim are the rules written in your policy, and two neighbors with different carriers may have slightly different clauses.</p><p>Still, most clauses follow a familiar pattern. Once you understand the usual parts, it becomes much easier to read your own and spot what is different about it.</p><h2>The parts of a typical clause</h2><p>Here is how a common appraisal clause is built, piece by piece, in plain terms.</p><h3>The trigger: a disagreement on the amount of loss</h3><p>Most clauses open with language such as “if you and we fail to agree on the amount of loss.” That phrase matters. It tells you appraisal is for disputes about value, not about whether the policy covers the damage. If your carrier denied the claim because it says the damage was wear and tear, or came from an excluded cause, appraisal may not be available until that coverage issue is resolved.</p><h3>The written demand</h3><p>Either party can usually make a written demand for appraisal. A clear letter that names the claim, states the disagreement and invokes the clause is typically enough. Keep proof of when it was sent and received, because the next deadlines often run from that date.</p><h3>Choosing appraisers</h3><p>Each side then selects a “competent” and “disinterested” or “independent” appraiser, often within 20 days of the demand. The policy controls the exact window. The appraiser you pick works on your side of the valuation, but the clause expects that person to be qualified and free of a stake in the outcome that the policy forbids.</p><h3>Selecting the umpire</h3><p>The two appraisers then have a set period, often 15 days, to agree on an umpire. If they can’t, the clause typically lets either party ask a court of record to appoint one. The umpire only steps in on items the appraisers can’t settle.</p><h3>The award</h3><p>Most clauses say that a written agreement signed by any two of the three (both appraisers, or one appraiser and the umpire) sets the amount of loss. Many policies ask the panel to state the replacement cost and actual cash value separately.</p><h3>Costs</h3><p>Each side usually pays its own appraiser and shares the umpire’s fee and other appraisal costs equally. Read this part closely, because some forms vary.</p><div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Find your clause first</b><p>Look in the “Conditions” or “Section I Conditions” part of your policy. Commercial and condo association forms may put it under “Loss Conditions.” Endorsements can change it, so read those too.</p></div><h2>Wording differences worth noticing</h2><p>Small changes in wording can matter. As you read your policy, look for:</p><ul><li><strong>Who can demand.</strong> Most clauses let either side invoke appraisal; a few have changed that or added conditions.</li><li><strong>Timeframes.</strong> The 20-day and 15-day windows are common, not universal.</li><li><strong>Appraiser standards.</strong> Some forms say “independent,” others “disinterested,” and some add requirements about fees or relationships.</li><li><strong>Carrier rights reserved.</strong> Many clauses say the carrier keeps its right to deny the claim even after an award. That reflects the line between amount and coverage.</li><li><strong>Pre-conditions.</strong> Some policies require a proof of loss, inspection access or an examination under oath before appraisal can go forward.</li></ul><p>If any of this language seems to block you from appraisal or shortens a deadline, confirm with a Mississippi attorney how it applies. State law generally restricts contracts from shortening statutory limitation periods, but how that works for a particular policy is a legal question.</p><h2>What the clause doesn’t do</h2><p>It helps to be clear about the edges of appraisal so expectations stay realistic:</p><ul><li>It doesn’t decide coverage, exclusions or causation disputes that depend on policy wording.</li><li>It doesn’t decide bad faith or award punitive damages. In Mississippi, those are court matters, and punitive damages are governed by statute.</li><li>It doesn’t stop your general limitation period from running. Mississippi’s general period for many contract claims is three years, but confirm your deadline with a Mississippi attorney.</li><li>It doesn’t override your deductible or prior payments. Those are applied after the award.</li></ul><h2>A quick reading exercise for your own policy</h2><p>Set aside twenty minutes with the full policy, not just the declarations page. Find the appraisal paragraph and underline the trigger phrase, every number of days, the words used to describe the appraisers, and the sentence about who pays. Then flip to the endorsements and search for the word “appraisal” to see whether any of them change the base form. Note the date your claim was reported and the date of the carrier’s last letter. With those few facts written down, you can have a much clearer conversation with an appraiser or an attorney about what comes next.</p><h2>Deciding whether to use it</h2><p>Appraisal tends to fit most often when the carrier has accepted the claim and the fight is about scope, quantities or price: how many squares of roofing, whether a whole elevation of siding must be replaced to match, or what local labor actually costs after a storm. It is less useful when the carrier says nothing is owed at all.</p><p>Before you send a demand, it is worth having someone compare the carrier’s estimate to the actual damage. Our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> does that, and the <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a> helps you lay out the policy’s windows once the clock starts. For a stage-by-stage view of the whole process, see our <a href="/appraisal-roadmap/">appraisal roadmap</a>, or read how <a href="/our-appraisal-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> works with us.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/coast-hurricane-deductibles-explained/"><small>Guide</small><span>Coast Hurricane Deductibles: The Math Behind a Named Storm Claim</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/documenting-tornado-damage/"><small>Guide</small><span>After the Siren: Documenting Tornado Damage in Mississippi</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/ice-storm-tree-claims-north-mississippi/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Storm Tree Claims in North Mississippi: Limits, Roofs and Pipes</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>Free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm CT</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-roadmap/">Appraisal roadmap</a></li><li><a href="/questions-answered/">Questions answered</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">Insurance claim terms</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li></ul></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Free calculators</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/claim-calculator-suite/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">Appraisal Payoff Estimator</a></li><li><a href="/claim-calculator-suite/holdback-recovery-calculator/">Holdback Recovery Calculator</a></li><li><a href="/claim-calculator-suite/roof-pitch-squares-calculator/">Roof Pitch &amp; Squares Calculator</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="band"><div class="wrap band-in rv"><div><h2>Not sure the carrier’s number is right?</h2><p>Send the estimate and a few photos. We’ll review it and tell you what we see — no obligation.</p></div><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
